{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fb5fa55c5G5SUVoyHBcPSE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n2\t# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n3\t#\n4\t# Go Style Constitution\n5\t#\n6\t# This constitution defines Go coding standards and design patterns for the\n7\t# project. It is injected into the stitch prompt alongside the execution\n8\t# constitution so Claude knows how to write idiomatic, maintainable Go code.\n9\t\n10\tcopyright_header: |\n11\t  Every Go file must start with the SPDX copyright header before the package\n12\t  declaration:\n13\t\n14\t    // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n15\t    // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n16\t\n17\t  This applies to hand-written files (magefiles/) and generated files (cmd/,\n18\t  pkg/, internal/, tests/). The header must appear on lines 1-2, followed by a\n19\t  blank line, then the package clause.\n20\t\n21\t\n22\tduplication: |\n23\t  Before writing a function, search for existing code that does the same thing.\n24\t  Before adding a field, check whether a struct already carries it. When two\n25\t  pieces of code share logic, extract the common part. Use struct embedding to\n26\t  share fields. Use helper functions to share behavior. Use interfaces to share\n27\t  contracts. The threshold is two: if you write the same thing twice, extract it.\n28\t\n29\timplementation_strategy: |\n30\t  Write all source files before running any build or test commands. Plan the\n31\t  full implementation (types, functions, imports) mentally, then write each\n32\t  file in dependency order. Only run `go build` or `go vet` after all files\n33\t  are written. This minimizes turns and avoids incremental compile-fix loops\n34\t  that re-send the entire prompt context on each iteration.\n35\t\n36\t  When writing a new cmd/ utility:\n37\t  1. Read the SRD and existing pkg/ contracts.\n38\t  2. Write main.go with all flag parsing, logic, and error handling in one pass.\n39\t  3. Write the test file with all test cases in one pass.\n40\t  4. Run `go vet` once to verify compilation.\n41\t  5. Run `go test` once to verify behavior.\n42\t  6. Fix any issues in a single edit pass.\n43\t\n44\t  Common Go compilation errors to avoid on first write:\n45\t  - Missing imports: always include fmt, os, io, strings when doing I/O.\n46\t  - Unused imports: do not import packages speculatively.\n47\t  - Type mismatches: check pkg/ contract signatures before calling.\n48\t  - Missing error handling: every function that returns error must be checked.\n49\t  - Unused variables: do not declare variables before they are needed.\n50\t\n51\tdesign_patterns:\n52\t  - name: Strategy\n53\t    description: |\n54\t      Use when multiple implementations of the same operation exist and the\n55\t      caller picks one. Define a small interface. Each implementation is a struct\n56\t      (or a function type) satisfying that interface. The caller selects at\n57\t      configuration time, not with if chains.\n58\t    symptoms: |\n59\t      A switch or if/else ladder choosing between behaviors, a function parameter\n60\t      named mode or kind, boolean flags that toggle logic branches.\n61\t\n62\t  - name: Command\n63\t    description: |\n64\t      Use when an action needs to be stored, queued, undone, or composed. Each\n65\t      command is a struct with an Execute() method. Commands can carry undo logic,\n66\t      be chained into sequences, or be logged for replay.\n67\t    symptoms: |\n68\t      Inline exec.Command calls scattered across a function, repeated sequences of\n69\t      shell operations that differ only in arguments, actions that should be\n70\t      retryable or reversible.\n71\t\n72\t  - name: Facade\n73\t    description: |\n74\t      Use when a caller should not know about the internal steps of a multi-step\n75\t      process. The facade exposes one method; internally it orchestrates several\n76\t      components. Do not add toggle parameters to a facade. If the caller needs to\n77\t      control sub-steps, they should use the components directly.\n78\t    symptoms: |\n79\t      A function that grew boolean parameters to skip internal steps, a caller that\n80\t      must call three functions in a specific order, an Init/Do/Cleanup pattern that\n81\t      keeps getting duplicated.\n82\t\n83\t  - name: Factory\n84\t    description: |\n85\t      Use NewXxx() functions to construct objects. Return concrete types but accept\n86\t      interfaces as dependencies. Factories centralize validation and wiring so\n87\t      callers never construct partially initialized structs.\n88\t\n89\t  - name: Decorator\n90\t    description: |\n91\t      Use to add cross-cutting concerns (logging, timing, retries) without\n92\t      modifying the wrapped object. In Go, the decorator accepts and returns the\n93\t      same interface. HTTP middleware is the canonical example.\n94\t    symptoms: |\n95\t      Logging or timing code copy-pasted around every call to an interface, optional\n96\t      behavior toggled by a boolean that wraps the real logic.\n97\t\n98\t  - name: Builder\n99\t    description: |\n100\t      Use when an object has many optional fields and constructing it in one call is\n101\t      unwieldy. Prefer the Functional Options variant (WithXxx functions) for\n102\t      idiomatic Go over a mutable builder struct.\n103\t\n104\t  - name: Adapter\n105\t    description: |\n106\t      Use when existing code does not satisfy the interface a consumer expects.\n107\t      Write a thin wrapper that translates the foreign API into the local interface.\n108\t      Do not modify the foreign code.\n109\t\n110\t  - name: Observer\n111\t    description: |\n112\t      Use channels for asynchronous event notification between goroutines. One\n113\t      producer writes to a channel; multiple consumers listen. Prefer this over\n114\t      callback registration when the producer should not know about its consumers.\n115\t\n116\tinterfaces: |\n117\t  Every boundary between a parent package and its internal sub-packages must be\n118\t  defined by an interface. The parent package declares the interface; the internal\n119\t  sub-package provides a concrete type that satisfies it. This applies even when\n120\t  only one implementation exists today. The purpose is testability and explicit\n121\t  contracts at every package boundary, not speculative abstraction.\n122\t\n123\t  Accept interfaces as parameters; return concrete structs. Keep interfaces small:\n124\t  one to three methods. A large interface is a sign that the abstraction is wrong.\n125\t  Split it into focused interfaces and compose them.\n126\t\n127\t  Do not use interface{} or any as function parameters, return types, or struct\n128\t  fields. Every value must have a concrete type or a named interface. If a\n129\t  function needs to accept multiple types, define an interface that captures the\n130\t  shared behavior, or use generics with type constraints. Type assertions and\n131\t  type switches on interface{}/any are symptoms of a missing abstraction. The\n132\t  only acceptable uses are stdlib boundaries that require it (e.g., json.Marshal,\n133\t  fmt.Sprintf variadic args).\n134\t\n135\tstruct_and_function_design: |\n136\t  Each struct represents one concept. Each function does one thing. If a function\n137\t  takes more than three parameters, group related parameters into a config struct.\n138\t  If a function exceeds 200 lines, find a seam and split it. Name structs and\n139\t  functions by what they represent or do, not by how they are called. Avoid\n140\t  generic names (Manager, Handler, Helper, Processor) unless the struct genuinely\n141\t  manages, handles, or processes a well-defined resource.\n142\t\n143\treceiver_conventions: |\n144\t  Use a pointer receiver when the method mutates the receiver, when the struct\n145\t  contains a mutex or sync primitive, or when the struct is large enough that\n146\t  copying it on every call is wasteful. Use a value receiver for small, immutable\n147\t  types where copying is cheap and the method does not modify the value. All\n148\t  methods on a given type must use the same receiver kind — do not mix pointer\n149\t  and value receivers on one type.\n150\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EvbyBN5pojWwdf88xtaVcD","session_id":"5e095c5d-1c7d-403b-b32d-ee3c441f5bea","uuid":"f5adf3db-51be-4848-b1e3-6e774955040a","timestamp":"2026-05-22T23:12:20.561Z"}
