{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_019HDbEMeLXWsCmH76d3Rt1s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U6hrZ1ELwf5jEdFo8gG6fR","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5475/docs/constitutions/execution.yaml","limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2269,"cache_read_input_tokens":40590,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2269,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":38,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EvbyBN5pojWwdf88xtaVcD","session_id":"5e095c5d-1c7d-403b-b32d-ee3c441f5bea","uuid":"95b290ed-0193-49ba-896b-4e1027cd12e8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U6hrZ1ELwf5jEdFo8gG6fR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Execution Constitution\n2\t#\n3\t# This constitution governs the stitch phase: implementing a single task from\n4\t# the work queue. It is injected into the stitch prompt so Claude knows how to\n5\t# write code that matches project conventions and quality standards.\n6\t\n7\tarticles:\n8\t  - id: E1\n9\t    title: Specification-first\n10\t    rule: |\n11\t      Code must correspond to existing SRDs and architecture. Read the SRDs and\n12\t      ARCHITECTURE sections listed in Required Reading before writing code. Do\n13\t      not invent interfaces, types, or patterns not described in those docs.\n14\t\n15\t  - id: E2\n16\t    title: Traceability\n17\t    rule: |\n18\t      Commit messages must mention which SRDs (or aspects) are implemented.\n19\t      Example: \"Implement X (srd-feature-name R6-R7)\". Where useful (package or\n20\t      top-of-file comments), list the implemented SRDs.\n21\t\n22\t  - id: E3\n23\t    title: No scope creep\n24\t    rule: |\n25\t      Do NOT modify files outside the Files to Create/Modify list unless a\n26\t      requirement explicitly demands it. Do NOT add features, refactoring, or\n27\t      improvements beyond what the requirements specify. Complete the task as\n28\t      scoped, nothing more.\n29\t\n30\t  - id: E4\n31\t    title: Session completion\n32\t    rule: |\n33\t      Work is complete when all files are written and tests pass. Do NOT run\n34\t      any git commands. Git operations (add, commit, status, init) are handled\n35\t      externally by the orchestrator.\n36\t\n37\t  - id: E5\n38\t    title: Quality gates\n39\t    rule: |\n40\t      Every item in Acceptance Criteria must be verified. Run tests if the\n41\t      criteria require it. Do not skip any criterion.\n42\t\n43\t  - id: E6\n44\t    title: Non-goals enforcement\n45\t    rule: |\n46\t      Features listed in a SRD's non_goals must not be implemented. Do not\n47\t      parse, accept, or act on flags, options, or behaviors that appear in\n48\t      non_goals. If a requirement references a non_goal feature, treat it as\n49\t      a specification error and skip the requirement with a TODO comment\n50\t      citing the conflict.\n51\t\n52\t  - id: E7\n53\t    title: Skip ambiguous requirements\n54\t    rule: |\n55\t      If a requirement is too vague to implement completely, do not implement\n56\t      it partially. Skip the requirement entirely. Add a TODO comment citing\n57\t      the requirement ID and describing what is missing from the specification.\n58\t      A parsed-but-nonfunctional flag is worse than a missing flag.\n59\t\n60\t  - id: E8\n61\t    title: Function size limit\n62\t    rule: |\n63\t      No function may exceed 40 lines of code. This is a hard limit, not a\n64\t      guideline. If a function exceeds 40 lines, find a seam and split it\n65\t      before the task is considered complete. Common seams: validation logic,\n66\t      formatting logic, I/O operations, and branch-specific handling.\n67\t\n68\t  - id: E9\n69\t    title: File size limit\n70\t    rule: |\n71\t      Each Go source file should stay under 500 lines of production code.\n72\t      When a file exceeds this threshold, split by responsibility: main.go\n73\t      (entry point and arg parsing), format.go (output formatting), and\n74\t      additional files as needed. Test files follow the same split. pkg/\n75\t      packages follow the same 500-line threshold.\n76\t\n77\t  - id: E10\n78\t    title: DRY enforcement\n79\t    rule: |\n80\t      The duplication threshold is two. If you write the same logic twice,\n81\t      extract it into a shared function. Common violations: entry display\n82\t      logic duplicated across output modes, error formatting repeated in\n83\t      multiple functions, path construction logic copied between list and\n84\t      recurse paths. Before writing a function, search for existing code\n85\t      that does the same thing.\n86\t\n87\tcoding_standards:\n88\t  copyright_header: |\n89\t    Every Go file must start with the SPDX copyright header:\n90\t    // Copyright (c) 2026 Petar Djukic. All rights reserved.\n91\t    // SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\n92\t\n93\t  never_duplicate_code: |\n94\t    Before writing a function, search for existing code that does the same thing.\n95\t    When two pieces of code share logic, extract the common part. The threshold\n96\t    is two: if you write the same thing twice, extract it. Common violations:\n97\t    entry display logic duplicated across output modes, error formatting repeated\n98\t    in multiple functions, path construction logic copied between list and recurse\n99\t    paths. See article E10.\n100\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01EvbyBN5pojWwdf88xtaVcD","session_id":"5e095c5d-1c7d-403b-b32d-ee3c441f5bea","uuid":"62f62fb6-8a0a-4db0-a334-7f017c0ad001","timestamp":"2026-05-22T23:12:23.246Z"}
