nNow I'm working through the field width calculations for the output format, starting with float32 which needs about 16 characters to accommodate the longest possible values like \"-3.4028235e+38\". I'm calculating the maximum field width for float64 values—17 significant digits plus sign, decimal point, and a 5-character exponent gives 24 characters, so with the leading space that's 25 total. Now I need to verify this against what GNU od actually outputs for the maximum float32 value, which should be around 3.4028235e+38. I'm verifying the field widths for the floating-point formats check out, then moving on to handle the signed decimal formats—I need to figure out the maximum widths for d1, d2, and d4 based on their ranges. Finishing the octal calculation for size 8, then verifying the field widths across all formats—octal matches at 22 digits plus padding, hex follows the pattern of doubling size plus one, and now I'm checking how `nativeFieldWidth` maps each type specifier to its corresponding field width.\n\nWriting the formatting code... Writing the implementation... Writing the implementation... is correct. Now I'm ready to rewrite the main.go file completely. I'll keep the namedChars table as-is since it's working properly, then rebuild the formatting logic to use the column layout system, fix the signExtend function to take a size parameter instead of inferring from the value, switch to strconv.FormatFloat for proper float handling, and clean up the parsing code to eliminate unused parameter warnings.\n\nWriting Go code...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":873,"cache_read_input_tokens":56555,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":873},"output_tokens":30,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e095c5d-1c7d-403b-b32d-ee3